 :rationale
 "Hospital coordination is NOT residential care coordination. While both actors
  follow the same governor/phase/operation pattern, hospitals are clinically
  central — the hospital's core business IS clinical care delivery. Therefore
  this actor's scope must be even more conservatively restricted than the
  residential-care siblings (isic-871/872/873/879): strictly administrative/
  facility coordination, with maximally-conservative clinical-content scanning
  to prevent any patient-care/clinical-decision/diagnostic/treatment language
  whatsoever, even at the periphery. The test legitimate-facility-safety-concern-
  is-not-scope-excluded verifies that real facility issues (elevator down, spill
  on floor) are NOT self-blocked by this scanning. Patient-safety concerns (vital
  sign change, clinical emergency) are explicitly out-of-scope — they must route
  through actual clinical staff/systems, not this coordination actor."

 :alternative-approaches
 [{:name "Broader hospital scope"
   :rejected-because "Hospitals are clinically central. A broader scope would risk clinical decisions bleeding into coordination logic, violating the principle that this actor must be permanently clinically-read-only. Narrower is safer."}
  {:name "Single health-services actor covering all health verticals"
   :rejected-because "Residential care (isic-871/872/873/879) and hospitals have fundamentally different domain boundaries. Nursing facilities' primary business IS care coordination. Hospitals' primary business IS clinical delivery. They need separate actors with different scope tuning."}]
}
